  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": {
    "@es": "Habilidades",
    "@en": "Skills"
   },
   "texto": [
    {
     "@es": "**Can** expresa una habilidad: **I can swim** (sé nadar). La forma es igual con todas las personas: **she can swim**, sin añadir **-s**.",
     "@en": "**Can** expresses a skill: **I can swim** (I can swim). The shape is the same with all people: **she can swim**, without adding **-s**."
    },
    {
     "@es": "En la negativa usamos **can't**: **I can't drive**. Para preguntar por una habilidad, ponemos **can** delante: **Can you drive?** — **Yes, I can / No, I can't**. En una situación, **Can you help me, please?** es una petición («¿puedes ayudarme, por favor?»), no una pregunta sobre una habilidad.",
     "@en": "In the negative we use **can't**: **I can't drive**. To ask for a skill, we put **can** in front of: **Can you drive?** ∞ **Yes, I can / No, I can't**. In a situation, **Can you help me, please?** is a request («Can you help me, please?»), not a question about a skill."
    },
    {
     "@es": "Después de **can** usamos el verbo sin **to** y no usamos **do**.",
     "@en": "After **can** we use the verb without **to** and do not use **do**."
    }
   ]
  },
